{"id": "BILL:cf_fcf", "ticker": "BILL", "qid": "cf_fcf", "answer_type": "text", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "Yes. BILL reports free cash flow as a non-GAAP liquidity measure defined as net cash provided by operating activities, adjusted by (i.e., less) purchases of property and equipment and capitalization of internal-use software costs. FY2026 free cash flow was $354.7 million ($354,676K) = operating cash flow $415.8M - purchases of property and equipment $3.3M - capitalized internal-use software $57.8M (FY2025: $312.5M). MUST include: that FCF is reported, the definition (operating cash flow less capex/purchases of property and equipment and capitalized internal-use software), and FY2026 FCF of ~$354.7M. OPTIONAL: components, FY2025 comparison, Q4 FCF $96.5M."}, "source": {"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "Free cash flow | $354,676 | $312,542 | $257,878", "quotes": [{"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "Free cash flow is a non-GAAP measure defined as net cash provided by operating activities, adjusted by purchases of property and equipment and capitalization of internal-use software costs."}, {"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "Net cash provided by operating activities | $415,832 | $350,644 | $278,771"}]}, "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "BILL:debt_instruments", "ticker": "BILL", "qid": "debt_instruments", "answer_type": "list", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "At June 30, 2026 BILL had four debt instruments outstanding (total carrying value $1,836.1M): (1) 2027 Convertible Senior Notes: $123.5M principal ($123,548K), 0% coupon (0.48% effective rate), due April 1, 2027 (classified current); (2) 2030 Convertible Senior Notes: $1.4B principal ($1,400,000K), 0% coupon (0.32% effective), due April 1, 2030; (3) 2021 Credit Facility (Divvy Peach / Goldman Sachs revolving facility secured by BILL Divvy Card receivables): $180.0M drawn of $300M commitment, SOFR (three-month) + 1.95% margin (6.43% effective at June 30, 2026), matures May 2028; (4) 2025 Credit Facility (Odin Financing / JPMorgan revolving facility secured by card receivables): $150.0M drawn of $300M commitment, SOFR (one-month) + 1.80% margin (6.05% effective), matures November 2027. MUST include all four instruments with principal, rate and maturity (for the revolvers, SOFR-plus-margin or the effective rate is acceptable). OPTIONAL: effective rates, conversion prices ($414.80 / $119.45), commitments, total $330.0M drawn. The 2025 Notes are NOT outstanding (remaining $33.5M settled in cash at maturity on December 1, 2025)."}, "source": {"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "As of June 30, 2026, we had outstanding $123.5 million aggregate principal amount of 0% convertible senior notes due April 1, 2027 (the 2027 Notes), and $1.4 billion aggregate principal amount of 0% convertible senior notes due April 1, 2030", "quotes": [{"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "2027 Notes, principal | 123,548 | — | 0.8 | 0.48%"}, {"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "2030 Notes, principal | 1,400,000 | 1,400,000 | 3.8 | 0.32%"}, {"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "2021 Credit Facility | 180,000 | — | 1.8 | 6.43%"}, {"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "2025 Credit Facility | 150,000 | — | 1.4 | 6.05%"}, {"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "The 2021 Credit Facility matures in May 2028."}, {"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "The 2025 Credit Facility matures in November 2027."}, {"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "Revolving loans under the 2021 Credit Facility bear interest at a rate per annum determined by reference to the applicable three-month secured overnight financing rate or a base rate, plus an applicable margin of 1.95%."}, {"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "Revolving loans under the 2025 Credit Facility bear interest at a rate per annum determined by reference to the applicable one-month secured overnight financing rate or a base rate, plus an applicable margin of 1.80%."}]}, "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "BILL:comp_competitors", "ticker": "BILL", "qid": "comp_competitors", "answer_type": "list", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "BILL says its primary competition is legacy manual processes used by SMBs. Other competitor categories: large firms focused on selling to enterprises (enterprise resource planning providers); firms with adjacent SMB products that enter its segments; point-solution providers (document management, workflow management, accounts payable, spend and expense management, card issuance, accounts receivable, electronic bill presentment and payment); companies offering industry-specific payment solutions; new/AI-native entrants offering AI-powered products; and software providers and financial-institution partners that may build or bundle competing products. Named competitors: Intuit (launched a native QuickBooks bill-pay solution in October 2023), Brex (recently acquired by Capital One) and Ramp (spend-management firms that introduced bill payment products). MUST include: legacy manual processes as primary competition, at least three of the competitor categories, and the named companies Intuit/QuickBooks, Brex and Ramp. OPTIONAL: Capital One's acquisition of Brex, key competitive factors."}, "source": {"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "Our primary competition remains the legacy manual processes that SMBs have relied upon for decades.", "quotes": [{"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "Other competitors range from large firms that predominantly focus on selling to enterprises; to firms focusing on adjacent products for SMBs which enter one or more of our market segments and offer products that compete with ours;"}, {"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "For example, in October 2023, Intuit launched a native bill payment solution with integration to its QuickBooks accounting software."}, {"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "In addition, Brex, recently acquired by Capital One, and Ramp, firms primarily known for offering spend and expense management products, introduced bill payment products in recent periods."}, {"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "Finally, new businesses may introduce AI-powered offerings seeking to compete with our platform."}]}, "note": "Item 1 lists only categories; the named companies (Intuit, Brex, Ramp) appear in the Item 1A competition risk factor.", "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "BILL:risk_key", "ticker": "BILL", "qid": "risk_key", "answer_type": "list", "grade": "support_only", "expected": {"text": "Reference (FY2026 10-K Item 1A), three BILL-specific risks: (1) Charge-card credit risk: BILL buys participation interests in the receivables from BILL Divvy Card spend from its Issuing Banks and bears the full credit risk if spending businesses fail to pay (invoice financing adds similar credit risk), so underwriting-model failures or SMB stress can drive charge-offs. (2) Customer-funds / float risk: BILL moves large sums of customer funds daily and earns interest on funds held for customers ($148.4M, ~9% of FY2026 revenue), exposing it to losses from fraud, errors or credit/market events on those funds and to revenue declines as interest rates fall. (3) Interchange dependence: a substantial portion of revenue is interchange fees on BILL Divvy Card and virtual card transactions, whose rates are set by card networks (Visa/Mastercard) and could be cut by network or regulatory changes. Other acceptable company-specific choices: fraud/risk-management failures on payments, dependence on Issuing Banks (Cross River, WEX Bank, WebBank) and card-receivable funding facilities, reliance on accounting-firm/financial-institution/software partners (e.g., Intuit), SMB concentration, payments/money-transmitter regulation."}, "source": {"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "Our BILL Divvy Card offering exposes us to credit risk and other risks related to spending businesses' ability to pay the balances incurred on their BILL Divvy Cards.", "quotes": [{"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "we purchase from the Issuing Banks participation interests in the accounts receivables generated when spending businesses make purchases using BILL Divvy Cards, and we bear the entire credit risk in the event that a spending business fails to pay card balances."}, {"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "We transfer large sums of customer funds daily, and are subject to numerous associated risks which could result in financial losses, damage to our reputation, or loss of trust in our brand, which would harm our business and financial results."}, {"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "For fiscal 2026, 2025, and 2024, we generated $148.4 million, $161.8 million, and $167.4 million, respectively, in revenue from interest earned on funds held in trust on behalf of customers while payment transactions were clearing,"}, {"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "A substantial portion of our revenue is derived from interchange fees, which exposes us to potential variability in income and other risks."}, {"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "The amount of interchange fees we earn is highly dependent upon the interchange rates set by the third-party card networks"}]}, "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "BILL:comp_bonus_metrics", "ticker": "BILL", "qid": "comp_bonus_metrics", "answer_type": "list", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "Per the 2025 proxy (fiscal 2025 program): Annual cash bonus (Fiscal 2025 Bonus Program) = Core Revenue (50% weight; target $1,300.0M) and EBITDA Less Float (50% weight; target $41.0M), with each NEO's payout adjustable up or down by up to 20% for individual performance (FY25 payout 125.1%). Performance-based equity: (a) Financial PSUs earned on one-year Core Revenue (100% weighting; target $1,300.0M) with a Non-GAAP Operating Income threshold ($190.7M) that must be exceeded to pay above target (payout 100.2%); (b) TSR PSUs earned on relative total stockholder return vs. the Russell 3000 index over a three-year period (July 1, 2024 - June 30, 2027). MUST include: Core Revenue and EBITDA Less Float for the bonus; Core Revenue (with Non-GAAP Operating Income guardrail) and relative TSR for PSUs. OPTIONAL: weights, targets, payouts, individual-performance modifier, definition of EBITDA Less Float."}, "source": {"doc_id": "BILL_DEF14A_2025", "quote": "Annual Cash Incentive | Annual cash incentives for executives require achievement of annual goals tied to Core Revenue (a key measure of the growth of our business) and EBITDA Less Float (to encourage efficient performance and execution across the Company), weighted equally.", "quotes": [{"doc_id": "BILL_DEF14A_2025", "quote": "FY25 Financial PSUs Program | Core Revenue - weighted 100% Non-GAAP Operating Income (to pay above target)"}, {"doc_id": "BILL_DEF14A_2025", "quote": "FY25 TSR PSUs Program | Relative Total Stockholder Return"}, {"doc_id": "BILL_DEF14A_2025", "quote": "Our Fiscal 2025 TSR PSUs are eligible to be earned based on our TSR relative to the Russell 3000 index (Index), with a single three-year performance period, beginning July 1, 2024 and ending June 30, 2027."}, {"doc_id": "BILL_DEF14A_2025", "quote": "each Named Executive Officer’s payout may be adjusted up or down by a maximum of 20% based on an evaluation of each executive’s individual performance during the year."}]}, "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "BILL:gov_structure", "ticker": "BILL", "qid": "gov_structure", "answer_type": "text", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "Yes, the board is classified: directors are divided into three classes serving staggered three-year terms (Class III directors elected at the 2025 annual meeting for terms expiring 2028; Class I and II terms expire in 2026 and 2027); this provision is still described in the FY2026 10-K. No, BILL has a single class of common stock ($0.00001 par, NYSE: BILL) with one vote per share and no dual-class structure; 10 million shares of undesignated preferred stock are authorized but none are issued. MUST include: classified/staggered board = yes; single class of common stock with one vote per share = no multiple voting classes. OPTIONAL: 13-member board, no cumulative voting, 66 2/3% supermajority provisions, blank-check preferred."}, "source": {"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "•establish that our board of directors is divided into three classes, with each class serving three-year staggered terms;", "quotes": [{"doc_id": "BILL_DEF14A_2025", "quote": "Our Board is divided into three classes with each class serving for three years and the terms of office of the respective classes expiring in successive years."}, {"doc_id": "BILL_DEF14A_2025", "quote": "Each share of common stock that you own represents one vote."}, {"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "Common Stock, $0.00001 par value | BILL | The New York Stock Exchange"}, {"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "Preferred stock: $ 0.00001 par value per share; 10,000 shares authorized; none issued and outstanding | — | —"}]}, "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "BILL:gov_holders", "ticker": "BILL", "qid": "gov_holders", "answer_type": "list", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "Per the 2025 proxy (ownership as of October 20, 2025; 100,378,848 shares outstanding), holders of more than 5%: The Vanguard Group, Inc. - 10,934,053 shares (10.9%); BlackRock, Inc. - 9,039,776 shares (9.0%); entities affiliated with Starboard Value LP - 8,639,900 shares (8.6%, incl. 1,614,152 shares under forward purchase contracts; also attributed to director Peter Feld); ER Collective Holdings, LLC - 7,668,300 shares (7.6%). MUST include all four holders (Vanguard, BlackRock, Starboard, ER Collective Holdings). OPTIONAL: share counts/percentages, the as-of date, CEO René Lacerte's 3.7% (below 5%, should not be listed as a 5% holder)."}, "source": {"doc_id": "BILL_DEF14A_2025", "quote": "The Vanguard Group, Inc. (15) | 10,934,053 | 10.9%", "quotes": [{"doc_id": "BILL_DEF14A_2025", "quote": "BlackRock, Inc. (16) | 9,039,776 | 9.0%"}, {"doc_id": "BILL_DEF14A_2025", "quote": "Entities affiliated with Starboard Value LP (17) | 8,639,900 | 8.6%"}, {"doc_id": "BILL_DEF14A_2025", "quote": "ER Collective Holdings, LLC (18) | 7,668,300 | 7.6%"}]}, "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "BILL:lit_material", "ticker": "BILL", "qid": "lit_material", "answer_type": "text", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "No. As of the FY2026 10-K (and the Q3 FY2026 10-Q), BILL states it is not currently a party to any legal proceedings it believes to be material to its business or financial condition; it is subject from time to time to ordinary-course claims (patent, commercial, product liability, employment, class action, whistleblower, regulatory) and its litigation reserve was immaterial at June 30, 2026. MUST include: no material pending legal proceedings. An answer describing a specific material lawsuit is wrong."}, "source": {"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "We are not currently a party to any legal proceedings that we believe to be material to our business or financial condition.", "quotes": [{"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "As of each of June 30, 2026 and 2025, the Company’s reserve for litigation is immaterial."}]}, "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "BILL:ma_recent", "ticker": "BILL", "qid": "ma_recent", "answer_type": "list", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "None. BILL did not complete any business acquisitions in FY2025 or FY2026: goodwill was unchanged at $2.4 billion at June 30, 2024, 2025 and 2026, the cash flow statements show no cash paid for acquisitions in either year (the last such outflow, $28.9M, was in FY2023), and acquisition and integration-related expenses were zero. The most recent acquisition cited is Finmark Financial, Inc. (completed November 2022, fiscal 2023). The only related item is a $2.9M purchase of intangible assets in FY2025, which is not a business combination. MUST include: no acquisitions completed in FY2025-FY2026 (an answer that no acquisitions are disclosed for the period is acceptable). WRONG: listing Finmark, Regpack, Invoice2go or Divvy as FY2025/FY2026 acquisitions or inventing consideration amounts. A not_found answer is also acceptable: the filings imply 'none' without stating it."}, "source": {"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "For example, in November 2022 we completed the acquisition of Finmark Financial, Inc. to augment our financial planning product offerings.", "quotes": [{"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "Goodwill, which is primarily attributable to expected synergies from acquisitions and is not deductible for U.S. federal and state income tax purposes, consisted of $ 2.4 billion as of each of June 30, 2026 and 2025."}, {"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2025", "quote": "Cash paid for acquisition, net of acquired cash and cash equivalents | — | — | (28,902)"}, {"doc_id": "BILL_8K_Q4FY2026", "quote": "Acquisition and integration-related expenses | — | — | — | —"}, {"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "Purchase of intangible assets | — | (2,868) | —"}]}, "note": "The answer 'none' is inferred from the absence of acquisitions (unchanged goodwill, zero acquisition cash outflows and acquisition expenses) rather than an explicit 'we made no acquisitions' statement.", "verified": "claude-draft", "accept_not_found": true}
{"id": "BILL:cap_buyback_auth", "ticker": "BILL", "qid": "cap_buyback_auth", "answer_type": "text", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "Authorizations: January 2023 - $300M (fully used by December 31, 2023); August 2024 - $300M (completed July 2025); December 2024 - an additional $200M approved in connection with the 2030 Notes offering (2,260,397 shares repurchased for $201.4M); August 2025 - $300M; May 2026 - up to $1.0 billion (the '2026 Share Repurchase Authorization', which includes unused amounts under the August 2025 program; 24-month term). As of June 30, 2026 (latest disclosure, FY2026 10-K), approximately $702.2 million ($702,170K) remained available. In FY2026 BILL repurchased 13.7M shares for $567.3M (8.4M shares / ~$300M in Q4). MUST include: the May 2026 $1.0B authorization and ~$702.2M remaining as of June 30, 2026. OPTIONAL: earlier programs, FY2026 repurchase activity, 24-month term."}, "source": {"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "2026 Share Repurchase Authorization | 8,434 | 300,473 | $35.63 | $702,170", "quotes": [{"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "In May 2026, we announced that our board of directors authorized the repurchase of up to $1.0 billion in shares of our outstanding common stock, which authorization includes unused amounts under the August 2025 Share Repurchase Program (the 2026 Share Repurchase Authorization)."}, {"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "June 1, 2026 - June 30, 2026 (3) | 6,254 | $34.21 | 6,254 | $702,170"}, {"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "In August 2025, the Company's board of directors authorized the repurchase of up to $ 300.0 million in shares of its outstanding common stock (the August 2025 Share Repurchase Program)."}, {"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "In August 2024, the Company's board of directors approved a share repurchase program, pursuant to which the Company announced its intention to purchase up to $ 300.0 million of its outstanding shares of common stock (the August 2024 Share Repurchase Program)."}, {"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "In addition, in December 2024, the Company's board of directors approved the repurchase of up to an additional $ 200.0 million of its outstanding shares of common stock in connection with the issuance of the 2030 Notes."}]}, "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "BILL:restructuring", "ticker": "BILL", "qid": "restructuring", "answer_type": "text", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "Yes. In FY2026 BILL executed three reductions in force: an October 15, 2025 RIF affecting ~6% of employees, a smaller RIF in March 2026 (no percentage disclosed), and a May 2026 RIF affecting ~30% of the workforce (majority of charges incurred in June 2026; substantially complete by end of Q1 FY2027 / September 30, 2026), alongside a shift to a remote-first model. Total FY2026 restructuring charges were $90.9 million ($90,895K), including $15.5M of accelerated stock-based compensation (Q4 FY2026 alone: $76.6M). MUST include: the RIFs with headcount impact (~6% in October 2025 and ~30% in May 2026) and total FY2026 charge of ~$90.9M. OPTIONAL: March 2026 smaller RIF (~$5.0M), October RIF charges ($9.3M), $56.2M restructuring liability at June 30, 2026, cash payments $19.2M, employee count falling from 2,364 (June 30, 2025) to 1,683 (June 30, 2026), the Q3 10-Q's earlier estimate of $30-60M for the May RIF."}, "source": {"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "During the year ended June 30, 2026, the Company recorded restructuring charges of $ 90.9 million, including $ 15.5 million of accelerated stock-based compensation expense.", "quotes": [{"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "On October 15, 2025, in furtherance of this commitment, the Company announced a RIF impacting approximately 6 % of employees. In March 2026, the Company undertook a smaller RIF and incurred additional restructuring charges."}, {"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "In May 2026, the Company announced an additional RIF impacting approximately 30 % of the Company's workforce, which will be substantially complete"}, {"doc_id": "BILL_8K_Q4FY2026", "quote": "Restructuring (1) | 76,569 | — | 90,895 | —"}, {"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2026", "quote": "As of June 30, 2026, we had a total of 1,683 employees, including those working in two offices in the U.S.: San Jose, CA, and Draper, UT; and others working remotely."}, {"doc_id": "BILL_10K_FY2025", "quote": "As of June 30, 2025, we had a total of 2,364 employees working in two offices in the U.S.: San Jose, CA, and Draper, UT; and others working remotely."}]}, "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "RPD:rev_streams", "ticker": "RPD", "qid": "rev_streams", "answer_type": "list", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "FY2025 (year ended Dec 31, 2025) total revenue was $859.8M ($859,794K), from two reported revenue streams: (1) Product subscriptions $831.3M ($831,325K; ~96.7% of revenue, +2.8% YoY) - cloud-based subscriptions, managed services (e.g., MDR), term software licenses with maintenance/support and content subscriptions; and (2) Professional services $28.5M ($28,469K; ~3.3% of revenue, -18.9% YoY) - deployment and training, incident response, penetration testing and security advisory services. MUST include: both streams (product subscriptions and professional services) with FY2025 amounts of ~$831.3M and ~$28.5M (or equivalent shares ~96.7% / ~3.3%). OPTIONAL: total $859.8M, YoY changes, prior-year figures ($808.9M / $35.1M in FY2024), description of what each stream comprises. The company does not report separate revenue amounts for cloud vs. managed services vs. term licenses."}, "source": {"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "Product subscriptions | $831,325 | $808,906 | $22,419 | 2.8%"}, "quotes": [{"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "Professional services | 28,469 | 35,101 | (6,632) | (18.9)%"}, {"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "Total revenue | $859,794 | $844,007 | $15,787 | 1.9%"}, {"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "We generate revenue primarily from: (1) product subscriptions from the sale of cloud-based subscriptions, managed services, term software licenses, content subscriptions and maintenance and support associated with our software licenses and (2) professional services"}], "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "RPD:rev_nrr", "ticker": "RPD", "qid": "rev_nrr", "answer_type": "percent", "grade": "abstain", "expected": {"not_found": true, "text": "Rapid7 does not disclose a net revenue retention or net dollar-based retention rate in any of the filings. Its disclosed key metrics are ARR ($824.0M at June 30, 2026, -2.0% YoY), number of customers (11,772) and ARR per customer ($70.0K). The 10-K only discusses renewal risk qualitatively (customers have no obligation to renew; renewal rates may decline or fluctuate) and lists strengthening the customer renewal rate as a strategy, without giving a rate. A correct answer says no NRR/NDR is disclosed."}, "source": {"doc_id": "RPD_10Q_Q2FY2026", "quote": "Annualized recurring revenue (“ARR”) | $824,020 | $840,610", "method": "grep -i across all 8 RPD filings for 'retention', 'net retention', 'dollar-based', 'net dollar', 'NRR', 'net revenue retention', 'expansion rate', 'gross retention', 'renewal rate'; read the Key Metrics sections of the FY2025 10-K, Q1/Q2 FY2026 10-Qs and the Q2 FY2026 8-K. Only ARR, customers and ARR per customer are disclosed; 'renewal rate' appears only in strategy/risk text without a figure."}, "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "RPD:rev_arr", "ticker": "RPD", "qid": "rev_arr", "answer_type": "currency", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "Yes. Rapid7 discloses annualized recurring revenue (ARR), defined as the annual value of all recurring revenue related to active contracts as of the last day of the period (excludes professional services). ARR was $824.0M ($824,020K) as of June 30, 2026, a 2.0% decrease year over year (from $840.6M at June 30, 2025). MUST include: yes/ARR disclosed and ~$824.0M as of June 30, 2026 (Q2 FY2026). OPTIONAL: -2.0% YoY; definition; FY2025 year-end ARR $839.9M ($839,850K, flat YoY); Q3 2026 guidance of ~$812M.", "value": 824020000, "unit": "USD", "period": "2026-06-30"}, "source": {"doc_id": "RPD_8K_Q2FY2026", "quote": "ARR: Annualized recurring revenue of $824.0 million, a decrease of 2.0% year-over-year."}, "quotes": [{"doc_id": "RPD_10Q_Q2FY2026", "quote": "Annualized recurring revenue (“ARR”) | $824,020 | $840,610"}, {"doc_id": "RPD_8K_Q2FY2026", "quote": "ARR is defined as the annual value of all recurring revenue related to active contracts as of the last day of the period."}], "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "RPD:rev_customers", "ticker": "RPD", "qid": "rev_customers", "answer_type": "count", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "Rapid7 reported 11,772 customers as of June 30, 2026 (up 1.1% from 11,643 a year earlier; 11,674 at Dec 31, 2025). A customer is defined as any entity that has an active Rapid7 recurring revenue contract as of the specified measurement date, excluding only InsightOps and Logentries customers with a contract value less than $2,400 per year. MUST include: 11,772 customers as of June 30, 2026 and the definition (active recurring revenue contract; excludes InsightOps/Logentries-only customers under $2,400/year). OPTIONAL: YoY change, ARR per customer ($70.0K), the press-release boilerplate 'more than 11,500 customers'.", "value": 11772, "unit": "count", "period": "2026-06-30"}, "source": {"doc_id": "RPD_10Q_Q2FY2026", "quote": "Number of customers | 11,772 | 11,643"}, "quotes": [{"doc_id": "RPD_10Q_Q2FY2026", "quote": "We define a customer as any entity that has an active Rapid7 recurring revenue contract as of the specified measurement date, excluding only InsightOps and Logentries customers with a contract value less than $2,400 per year."}], "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "RPD:rev_concentration", "ticker": "RPD", "qid": "rev_concentration", "answer_type": "yes_no", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "No. No single customer, including channel partners, accounted for 10% or more of total revenue in FY2025 (nor in 2024 or 2023), and none accounted for 10% or more of accounts receivable at Dec 31, 2025 or 2024. MUST include: 'No' for FY2025. OPTIONAL: the statement also covers channel partners and accounts receivable."}, "source": {"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "No single customer, including channel partners, accounted for 10% or more of our total revenues in 2025, 2024 or 2023"}, "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "RPD:rev_international", "ticker": "RPD", "qid": "rev_international", "answer_type": "percent", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "About 28.8% of FY2025 revenue came from customers outside the United States: 'Rest of world' revenue (by customer location) was $247.6M ($247,569K) of total revenue of $859.8M ($859,794K); the United States contributed $612.2M (~71.2%). MUST include: ~28.8% (accept ~29%, or the $247.6M rest-of-world figure with the share). OPTIONAL: US revenue $612.2M; the risk-factor statement that operations located outside North America generated 25% of FY2025 revenue (a different basis - operations location and North America - which should not be given as the answer to this question).", "value": 28.8, "unit": "percent", "period": "FY2025"}, "source": {"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "Rest of world | 247,569 | 229,922 | 198,682"}, "quotes": [{"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "United States | $612,225 | $614,085 | $579,025"}, {"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "Revenues by geographic area presented based upon the location of the customer are as follows (in thousands):"}], "note": "Computed: 247,569 / 859,794 = 28.79%. The 10-K risk factors separately say operations located outside North America generated 25% of revenue in 2025; that is a different measure (location of operations, North America not U.S.).", "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "RPD:op_nongaap_quarter", "ticker": "RPD", "qid": "op_nongaap_quarter", "answer_type": "currency", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "For Q2 FY2026 (quarter ended June 30, 2026), Rapid7 reported non-GAAP income from operations of $28.9M ($28,885K), versus GAAP income from operations of $3.0M ($3,019K). Non-GAAP operating margin was 13.7% (vs. $36.3M / 17.0% in Q2 2025). MUST include: ~$28.9M for the quarter ended June 30, 2026. OPTIONAL: GAAP operating income $3.0M; margin 13.7%; prior-year $36.3M; reconciling items (SBC $19.8M, amortization of acquired intangibles $4.3M, acquisition-related $0.1M, restructuring $1.7M).", "value": 28885000, "unit": "USD", "period": "Q2 FY2026 (three months ended 2026-06-30)"}, "source": {"doc_id": "RPD_8K_Q2FY2026", "quote": "Non-GAAP income from operations | $28,885 | $36,348 | $53,317 | $68,701"}, "quotes": [{"doc_id": "RPD_8K_Q2FY2026", "quote": "Operating Income: GAAP income from operations of $3.0 million; Non-GAAP income from operations of $28.9 million."}], "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "RPD:cf_fcf", "ticker": "RPD", "qid": "cf_fcf", "answer_type": "text", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "Yes. Rapid7 reports free cash flow, a non-GAAP measure defined as cash provided by operating activities less purchases of property and equipment and capitalization of internal-use software costs. FY2025 free cash flow was $130.1M ($130,122K) = operating cash flow $153.8M - capex $7.6M - capitalized internal-use software $16.1M (down from $154.1M in FY2024). MUST include: the definition (OCF less purchases of property and equipment and capitalized internal-use software) and ~$130.1M for FY2025. OPTIONAL: the components; FY2024 comparison; the company also reports unlevered free cash flow in its press release.", "value": 130122000, "unit": "USD", "period": "FY2025"}, "source": {"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "Free cash flow | $130,122 | $154,083 | $84,034"}, "quotes": [{"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "Free cash flow is a non-GAAP measure that we define as cash provided by operating activities less purchases of property and equipment and capitalization of internal-use software costs."}, {"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "Net cash provided by operating activities | $153,827 | $171,670 | $104,278"}], "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "RPD:debt_instruments", "ticker": "RPD", "qid": "debt_instruments", "answer_type": "list", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "At Dec 31, 2025: (1) 0.25% convertible senior notes due March 15, 2027 ('2027 Notes') - $600.0M principal outstanding (net carrying amount $597.0M); (2) 1.25% convertible senior notes due March 15, 2029 ('2029 Notes') - $300.0M principal (net carrying amount $295.3M). Both are senior unsecured. Total convertible principal $900.0M (net $892.3M). (3) A $200.0M senior secured revolving credit facility (Credit Agreement dated June 25, 2025, JPMorgan as agent) with no borrowings outstanding; borrowings bear interest at SOFR (floor 0) or alternate base rate plus a leverage-based margin, 0.20%-0.25% commitment fee on unused amounts; matures on the fifth anniversary of the June 25, 2025 closing (i.e., 2030), or 91 days before the earliest maturity of the 2027/2029 Notes if specified liquidity conditions are not met; $5.6M of letters of credit outstanding reduce availability. The 2.25% 2025 Notes matured and were repaid ($46.0M principal) on May 1, 2025, so were not outstanding at year-end. MUST include: both convertible note series with principal ($600M, $300M), coupons (0.25%, 1.25%) and maturities (March 15, 2027; March 15, 2029). OPTIONAL but creditable: the undrawn revolver; carrying amounts; effective rates (0.67%, 1.69%); 2025 Notes repayment."}, "source": {"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "Balance at December 31, 2025 | $— | $— | $— | $600,000 | $(3,044) | $596,956 | $300,000 | $(4,672) | $295,328"}, "quotes": [{"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "2027 Notes | March 15, 2027 | 0.25 % | September 15, 2021 | 0.67 % | March 15 and September 15 | 9.6734 | 103.38 | 5.8"}, {"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "2029 Notes | March 15, 2029 | 1.25 % | March 15, 2024 | 1.69 % | March 15 and September 15 | 15.4213 | 64.85 | 4.6"}, {"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "As of December 31, 2025, we did not have any outstanding borrowings under the Credit Agreement."}], "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "RPD:comp_competitors", "ticker": "RPD", "qid": "comp_competitors", "answer_type": "list", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "Rapid7 names categories of competitors, not specific companies. It says it primarily competes with established and emerging security product vendors, including: large companies that incorporate security products into their products; security platform providers; managed detection and response (MDR) service providers; XDR and SIEM vendors; cloud security vendors; exposure management vendors; vulnerability risk management vendors; application security vendors; threat intelligence vendors; and legacy security, systems management, MSSPs and other IT vendors. MUST include: that competitors are described by category (no named competitors) and most of these categories (at least MDR providers, XDR/SIEM vendors, vulnerability risk management/exposure management vendors, cloud security vendors, and large companies/platform providers). OPTIONAL: competitive factors (functionality, breadth, cost, etc.). Naming specific companies (e.g., from the proxy's compensation peer group or the 10-K's technology partners like Microsoft/Palo Alto Networks) as named competitors is not supported by the filings."}, "source": {"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "We primarily compete with established and emerging security product vendors, including the following:"}, "quotes": [{"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "We primarily compete with large companies that incorporate security products into their products, security platform providers, MDR service providers, XDR and SIEM vendors, cloud security vendors, exposure management vendors, vulnerability risk management vendors"}, {"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "•legacy security, systems management, MSSPs, and other IT vendors."}], "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "RPD:risk_key", "ticker": "RPD", "qid": "risk_key", "answer_type": "list", "grade": "support_only", "expected": {"text": "Reference summary (FY2025 10-K): (1) Product efficacy/defect risk - if Rapid7's security products and services fail to detect vulnerabilities or threats, generate false positives (e.g., from Metasploit's community-contributed exploits), or contain defects, its brand and reputation as a security vendor, customer renewals and liability exposure would suffer. (2) Being a cybersecurity vendor makes Rapid7 itself a prominent target: a breach of its own or its third-party providers' systems or customer data (heightened by Metasploit's profile among hackers) could harm reputation, reduce demand and create significant liabilities. (3) Renewal/expansion dependence - growth depends on customers renewing term subscriptions on equal or better terms and buying more (cross-sell/upsell), with no renewal obligation (notable given ARR was flat in FY2025). Other defensible company-specific choices: the $900M of convertible notes (including $600M due March 2027) and ability to repay; the shift to a consolidated Command Platform/AI offering; activist stockholder JANA (~10.1% owner with a board seat)."}, "source": {"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "Real or perceived failures, errors or defects in our solutions could adversely affect our brand and reputation, which could have an adverse effect on our business and results of operations."}, "quotes": [{"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "We sell cybersecurity and data analytics products. As a result, we have been and will continue to be a target of cyber attacks"}, {"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "Our business and growth depend substantially on customers renewing and expanding their subscriptions with us."}, {"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "If our products or service offerings fail to detect vulnerabilities in our customers’ cybersecurity infrastructure"}], "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "RPD:mgmt_ceo", "ticker": "RPD", "qid": "mgmt_ceo", "answer_type": "text", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "Wael Mohamed has been Chief Executive Officer since June 1, 2026 (offer letter dated May 27, 2026). He had served on Rapid7's board since April 2025. He succeeded Corey Thomas (CEO since October 2012), who became Executive Chairman effective June 1, 2026. MUST include: Wael Mohamed and June 1, 2026 (or 'since June 2026'). OPTIONAL: predecessor Corey Thomas / his new Executive Chairman role; Mohamed's prior roles (e.g., former CEO of Forescout). Answering Corey Thomas (CEO per the April 2026 proxy and FY2025 10-K) is outdated and incorrect for 'current'."}, "source": {"doc_id": "RPD_10Q_Q2FY2026", "quote": "Effective June 1, 2026, Wael Mohamed was appointed Chief Executive Officer, Corey Thomas was appointed Executive Chairman, and Marc Brown was appointed Lead Independent Director."}, "quotes": [{"doc_id": "RPD_8K_Q2FY2026", "quote": "said Wael Mohamed, CEO of Rapid7"}], "note": "CEO changed after the proxy: the April 2026 proxy lists Corey Thomas as CEO; the Q2 FY2026 10-Q and 8-K show Wael Mohamed as CEO effective June 1, 2026.", "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "RPD:comp_ceo_total", "ticker": "RPD", "qid": "comp_ceo_total", "answer_type": "currency", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "Corey Thomas (CEO during FY2025) had total FY2025 compensation of $10,058,576 in the Summary Compensation Table: salary $545,000, stock awards $9,195,576, non-equity incentive plan compensation $315,000, all other compensation $3,000 (no discretionary bonus). MUST include: ~$10.06M ($10,058,576) for Corey Thomas for 2025. OPTIONAL: components; prior years ($14,854,209 in 2024; $3,140,768 in 2023). Current CEO Wael Mohamed (appointed June 2026) has no entry in this proxy.", "value": 10058576, "unit": "USD", "period": "FY2025"}, "source": {"doc_id": "RPD_DEF14A_2026", "quote": "Corey Thomas Chief Executive Officer | 2025 | 545,000 | — | 9,195,576 | 315,000 | 3,000 | 10,058,576"}, "quotes": [{"doc_id": "RPD_DEF14A_2026", "quote": "Mr. Thomas' annual total compensation for 2025, as reported in the Summary Compensation Table included in this Proxy Statement, was $10,058,576."}], "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "RPD:comp_bonus_metrics", "ticker": "RPD", "qid": "comp_bonus_metrics", "answer_type": "list", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "For FY2025 (per the 2026 proxy): the annual cash bonus under the Executive Incentive Bonus Plan was based 50% on Non-GAAP Operating Income and 50% on ARR (annualized recurring revenue). Performance-based equity (PSUs, one-year performance period, then three-year vesting) was tied to Net ARR (net new ARR) and Adjusted EBITDA goals. MUST include: bonus = Non-GAAP Operating Income + ARR (equal weights); PSUs = (Net) ARR + Adjusted EBITDA. OPTIONAL: FY2025 outcomes - Non-GAAP Operating Income $135.7M met target (100%), ARR $839.8M missed the $870M threshold (0%), so bonus paid at 50% of target; PSUs earned at 50% (Adjusted EBITDA $161.9M met target, Net ARR $0.0M missed threshold); bonus capped at 125%, PSUs at 200%. Note the proxy text sometimes says PSUs were tied to 'ARR' while the payout table and 10-K specify 'Net ARR'; either phrasing is acceptable."}, "source": {"doc_id": "RPD_DEF14A_2026", "quote": "For 2025, 50% of the target annual performance bonus opportunity was based on the company achieving a target Non-GAAP Operating Income for the year and 50% was based on the Company achieving target ARR for the year."}, "quotes": [{"doc_id": "RPD_DEF14A_2026", "quote": "Each of the PSU awards granted to the named executive officers required the achievement of defined ARR and Adjusted EBITDA goals."}, {"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "In January 2025, our Compensation Committee awarded 181,067 PSUs that required the achievement of net annualized recurring revenue (“Net ARR”) and Adjusted EBITDA targets for the 2025 fiscal-year to earn any payout."}], "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "RPD:comp_sayonpay", "ticker": "RPD", "qid": "comp_sayonpay", "answer_type": "percent", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "At the 2025 Annual Meeting, 91% of stockholders approved the say-on-pay advisory vote on named executive officer compensation. MUST include: ~91% and that it refers to the 2025 annual meeting vote. (The proxy phrases it as '91% of our stockholders approved'.)", "value": 91, "unit": "percent", "period": "2025 Annual Meeting"}, "source": {"doc_id": "RPD_DEF14A_2026", "quote": "At our 2025 Annual Meeting, 91% of our stockholders approved, on an advisory basis, the compensation of our named executive officers."}, "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "RPD:gov_board", "ticker": "RPD", "qid": "gov_board", "answer_type": "text", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "Per the April 2026 proxy, the board had 11 members, 10 of whom were independent (all except CEO Corey Thomas): Bruner, Sondhi, Berry, Brown, Burns, Galligan, Holzman, Mohamed, Nye and Schodorf. The board nominated 11 candidates for 2026 (Michael Berry not standing; Jeff Kalowski, also determined independent, nominated), so the post-meeting slate is likewise 11 directors with 10 independent. MUST include: 11 directors and 10 independent. OPTIONAL: names; the Berry/Kalowski change; Marc Brown as independent Chairman. Later context (not required): Wael Mohamed became CEO on June 1, 2026, so he would no longer be independent after that date."}, "source": {"doc_id": "RPD_DEF14A_2026", "quote": "The Board presently has eleven members, each of whose term of office expires in 2026."}, "quotes": [{"doc_id": "RPD_DEF14A_2026", "quote": "the Board has affirmatively determined that the following ten directors and one director nominee are independent within the meaning of the applicable Nasdaq listing standards"}, {"doc_id": "RPD_DEF14A_2026", "quote": "Mr. Thomas is not an independent director by virtue of his employment with us."}], "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "RPD:gov_structure", "ticker": "RPD", "qid": "gov_structure", "answer_type": "text", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "No and no. The board is not classified: it has been declassified, and since the 2023 annual meeting each director is elected annually for a one-year term (all 11 nominees stand for election to terms expiring at the 2027 meeting). Rapid7 has a single class of common stock ($0.01 par, listed on Nasdaq as RPD) with one vote per share; there is no dual-class structure. 10,000,000 shares of preferred stock are authorized but none are issued or outstanding. MUST include: not staggered/annual one-year terms (declassified) AND single class of common stock / no multiple voting classes. OPTIONAL: blank-check preferred authorization; other anti-takeover provisions (no written consent, advance notice, Delaware Section 203)."}, "source": {"doc_id": "RPD_DEF14A_2026", "quote": "each director elected at each annual meeting of stockholders will hold office for a term of one year, expiring at the next annual meeting of stockholders"}, "quotes": [{"doc_id": "RPD_DEF14A_2026", "quote": "including declassifying our Board, eliminating supermajority voting provisions from our governance documents"}, {"doc_id": "RPD_DEF14A_2026", "quote": "On each matter to be voted upon, you have one vote for each share of common stock you own as of April 15, 2026."}, {"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "Common Stock, par value $0.01 per share | RPD | The Nasdaq Global Market"}], "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "RPD:gov_activist", "ticker": "RPD", "qid": "gov_activist", "answer_type": "text", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "Yes. Rapid7 entered into a Cooperation Agreement with activist investor JANA Partners Management, LP dated March 21, 2025, and a Nomination and Support Agreement with JANA dated March 26, 2026 (both filed as exhibits). The filings in this corpus identify these agreements only by title in the exhibit indexes and do not summarize their terms. Related disclosed facts: JANA partner Kevin Galligan joined the board in April 2025 (effective April 22, 2025) and serves on the Compensation Committee; JANA reported ~10.1% beneficial ownership (6,760,149 shares per its March 30, 2026 Schedule 13D/A); the 10-K cites JANA as an example in its activist-stockholder risk factor. MUST include: yes, with JANA Partners, and at least one of the two agreements (ideally both with dates). SHOULD note that detailed terms are not described in the filings; the Galligan board seat and ~10.1% stake are the key disclosed facts. Specific standstill/voting terms asserted without support from the filings should not be credited."}, "source": {"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "10.29 | Cooperation Agreement, by and between Rapid7, Inc. and JANA Partners Management, LP, dated March 21, 2025."}, "quotes": [{"doc_id": "RPD_10Q_Q1FY2026", "quote": "10.2 | Nomination and Support Agreement, by and between Rapid7, Inc. and JANA Partners Management, LP, dated March 26, 2026"}, {"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "Kevin Galligan, a Partner and Director of Research at JANA, has also served as a member of our Board since April 2025."}, {"doc_id": "RPD_DEF14A_2026", "quote": "Entities affiliated with JANA Partners Management, LP (1) | 6,760,149 | 10.1%"}], "note": "Agreement terms (e.g., board appointments, standstill) are not described in the corpus text; only exhibit titles, the Galligan appointment, and JANA's stake are disclosed.", "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "RPD:gov_holders", "ticker": "RPD", "qid": "gov_holders", "answer_type": "list", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "Per the 2026 proxy (ownership as of March 31, 2026, 66,770,824 shares outstanding), two holders owned more than 5%: entities affiliated with JANA Partners Management, LP - 6,760,149 shares (10.1%); and entities affiliated with BlackRock, Inc. - 5,913,948 shares (8.9%). MUST include: JANA (~10.1%) and BlackRock (~8.9%) and no others. OPTIONAL: share counts; source filings (JANA 13D/A of March 30, 2026; BlackRock 13G/A of October 17, 2025)."}, "source": {"doc_id": "RPD_DEF14A_2026", "quote": "Entities affiliated with JANA Partners Management, LP (1) | 6,760,149 | 10.1%"}, "quotes": [{"doc_id": "RPD_DEF14A_2026", "quote": "Entities affiliated with BlackRock Inc. (2) | 5,913,948 | 8.9%"}], "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "RPD:lit_material", "ticker": "RPD", "qid": "lit_material", "answer_type": "text", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "No. Rapid7 does not disclose any material pending legal proceedings. Its most recent 10-Q (Q2 FY2026) and the FY2025 10-K state only that it is from time to time party to litigation or claims incident to the ordinary course of business and believes the outcome of these ordinary course matters will not have a material adverse effect on its business, financial condition or results of operations. MUST include: no material pending proceedings (only ordinary-course matters). OPTIONAL: standard indemnification obligations."}, "source": {"doc_id": "RPD_10Q_Q2FY2026", "quote": "we currently believe that the final outcome of these ordinary course matters will not have a material adverse effect on our business, financial condition or results of operations"}, "quotes": [{"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "From time to time, we are a party to litigation or subject to claims incident to the ordinary course of business."}], "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "RPD:ma_recent", "ticker": "RPD", "qid": "ma_recent", "answer_type": "list", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "In the two most recent fiscal years (FY2024-FY2025), Rapid7 completed one acquisition: Noetic Cyber, Inc. (cyber asset attack surface management), acquired July 3, 2024, for purchase consideration with an aggregate fair value of $51.2M - $38.6M cash paid at closing, $12.1M contingent (earnout) consideration and $0.5M deferred cash (plus up to $20.0M total earnout potential, of which $6.9M employment-contingent stock is treated as compensation). No acquisitions were completed in FY2025 (business acquisitions, net of cash acquired: none in 2025 vs. $37.3M in 2024). MUST include: Noetic (July 2024) with ~$51.2M total consideration, and that there were no acquisitions in FY2025. OPTIONAL: consideration breakdown; subsequent event - Kenzo Security, Inc. acquired March 26, 2026 for $25.5M ($24.2M cash + $1.3M deferred), which is after FY2025 and should not be presented as an FY2024/FY2025 acquisition; Minerva Labs (March 2023, $34.6M) is before the window."}, "source": {"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "The purchase consideration consisted of $ 38.6 million in cash paid at closing, $ 12.1 million of contingent consideration and $ 0.5 million of deferred cash payments."}, "quotes": [{"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "Fair value of total consideration transferred | $51,152"}, {"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "Business acquisitions, net of cash acquired | — | (37,301) | (34,841)"}, {"doc_id": "RPD_10Q_Q2FY2026", "quote": "On March 26, 2026, we acquired all of the equity interest of Kenzo Security, Inc. (\"Kenzo\"), a United States-based agentic AI security platform built to scale autonomous security investigations, for a purchase price with an aggregate fair value of $ 25.5 million."}], "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "RPD:cap_buyback_auth", "ticker": "RPD", "qid": "cap_buyback_auth", "answer_type": "text", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "No share repurchase program or board repurchase authorization is disclosed in any of the filings, so no remaining authorization amount exists to report. Related facts: the FY2025 10-K Item 5 and the Q1/Q2 FY2026 10-Qs report 'None' for issuer purchases of equity securities; the FY2025 cash flow statement has no stock repurchase line; treasury stock was unchanged at 570,255 shares. The only 'repurchase' references concern convertible notes (e.g., the 2023 partial repurchase of 2025 Notes; holders' fundamental-change put rights). A correct answer states that no buyback authorization is disclosed / the company has no repurchase program. A not_found answer is also acceptable: the filings imply 'none' without stating it."}, "source": {"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "Purchase of Equity Securities by the Issuer and Affiliated Purchasers", "method": "grep -i across all 8 RPD filings for 'repurchase', 'buyback', 'buy back', 'repurchase program', 'authorized', 'Issuer Purchases'; read FY2025 10-K Item 5, Q1/Q2/Q3 10-Q Part II Item 2, the FY2025 and Q2 FY2026 cash flow statements, and the 8-K. All repurchase mentions relate to convertible notes; Item 5/Part II Item 2 issuer purchases are 'None'."}, "quotes": [{"doc_id": "RPD_10Q_Q2FY2026", "quote": "(c) Issuer Purchases of Equity Securities"}, {"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "Treasury stock, at cost, 570,255 shares at December 31, 2025 and 2024 | (4,765) | (4,765)"}], "note": "Judgment call: the question presupposes an authorization. None is disclosed (and no repurchases were made), so graded as abstain; an answer saying 'no repurchase authorization/program is disclosed' should count as correct.", "verified": "claude-draft", "accept_not_found": true}
{"id": "RPD:cap_buyback_spend", "ticker": "RPD", "qid": "cap_buyback_spend", "answer_type": "currency", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "$0 - Rapid7 did not repurchase any common stock in FY2025. The FY2025 cash flow statement has no stock repurchase outflow (financing activities consisted of the $46.0M 2025 Notes maturity payment, $4.1M Noetic earnout, $3.0M taxes on net share settlement, $1.7M debt issuance costs, offset by ESPP and option proceeds), and Item 5 reports no issuer purchases of equity securities. MUST include: none / $0 for FY2025. Answers citing the $3.0M of taxes paid for net share settlement of equity awards as 'buybacks' are incorrect.", "value": 0, "unit": "USD", "period": "FY2025"}, "source": {"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "Financing activities used $45.5 million for the year ended December 31, 2025, which consisted primarily of $46.0 million of cash paid relating to the repayment of our 2025 convertible senior note"}, "quotes": [{"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "Purchase of Equity Securities by the Issuer and Affiliated Purchasers"}, {"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "Taxes paid related to net share settlement of equity awards | (3,020) | (4,730) | (5,570)"}], "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "RPD:emp_count", "ticker": "RPD", "qid": "emp_count", "answer_type": "count", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "Rapid7 had 2,613 full-time employees as of December 31, 2025 (382 product and service delivery and support, 986 sales and marketing, 888 research and development, 357 general and administrative; 1,180 in the U.S. and 1,433 internationally). MUST include: 2,613 as of Dec 31, 2025. OPTIONAL: functional / geographic breakdown.", "value": 2613, "unit": "count", "period": "2025-12-31"}, "source": {"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "As of December 31, 2025, we had 2,613 full-time employees"}, "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "RPD:restructuring", "ticker": "RPD", "qid": "restructuring", "answer_type": "text", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "No. Rapid7 did not undertake a restructuring in FY2025 and recorded no restructuring expense in 2025. Its prior plan (approved August 2023, ~16% workforce reduction, $22.2M of charges in 2023) was completed in Q1 2024 (a $(0.2)M adjustment in 2024), with no remaining liability. Subsequent to FY2025: in June 2026 it executed a limited reduction in force (mainly sales and marketing), recording $1.7M of charges in Q2 2026, and on August 7, 2026 the board approved a 2026 Restructuring Plan reducing the workforce by ~12%, with estimated charges of ~$10-11M (mainly severance) expected in Q3-Q4 2026. MUST include: no restructuring (no charges) in FY2025. OPTIONAL: the 2023 plan history and the 2026 plan (~12%, $10-11M) if clearly labeled as occurring after FY2025. An answer presenting the 2026 plan as the FY2025 restructuring is incorrect."}, "source": {"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "Restructuring | — | — | 22,227"}, "quotes": [{"doc_id": "RPD_10Q_Q2FY2026", "quote": "whereas, there were no restructuring expenses in 2025"}, {"doc_id": "RPD_10K_FY2025", "quote": "During the first quarter of 2024, the execution of the Restructuring Plan was completed"}, {"doc_id": "RPD_10Q_Q2FY2026", "quote": "The 2026 Restructuring Plan includes reduction of the Company’s workforce by approximately 12 %."}, {"doc_id": "RPD_10Q_Q2FY2026", "quote": "We estimate that we will incur approximately $ 10 million - $ 11 million in charges in connection with the 2026 Restructuring Plan"}], "note": "Period is FY2025 (no restructuring). A 2026 plan exists; answers may mention it as subsequent context.", "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "RPD:na_projections", "ticker": "RPD", "qid": "na_projections", "answer_type": "text", "grade": "abstain", "expected": {"not_found": true, "text": "The filings do not disclose management's internal revenue projections for the next five fiscal years. The only forward-looking revenue figures are public guidance in the Q2 FY2026 earnings release: Q3 2026 revenue of $208-$210M and full-year 2026 revenue of $837-$841M (plus ARR ~$812M for Q3 2026, non-GAAP operating income and ~$130M FY2026 free cash flow). No multi-year or five-year projections appear in the 10-Ks, 10-Qs, 8-K or proxy (the proxy notes 2025 bonus targets were set above the internal plan but does not disclose the plan)."}, "source": {"doc_id": "RPD_8K_Q2FY2026", "quote": "Revenue | $208 | to | $210 | $837 | to | $841", "method": "grep -i across all 8 RPD filings for 'projection', 'forecast', 'five-year', 'five year', 'long-term target', 'financial targets', 'outlook', 'internal plan', 'multi-year plan', 'guidance'; read the 8-K guidance section. Only FY2026/Q3 2026 guidance exists."}, "verified": "claude-draft"}
// BOX (held-out) golden items, drafted after the system freeze; quotes machine-verified.
{"id": "BOX:rev_streams", "ticker": "BOX", "qid": "rev_streams", "answer_type": "list", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "Box derives revenue from three sources: (1) subscription revenue (fees for access to the Box ICM platform, including routine customer support); (2) premier services packages; and (3) professional services (implementation/best-practice consulting, project management). Box does NOT disclose revenue by stream in dollars: it states that practically all revenue comes from subscription and premier services and that professional services revenue is not material. Total FY2026 (year ended Jan 31, 2026) revenue was $1,177.3 million ($1,177,253 thousand), up 8% from $1,090.1 million. MUST: name the three streams; give total FY2026 revenue (~$1.18B); say that no per-stream dollar split is disclosed (subscription + premier = practically all revenue, professional services immaterial). An answer that invents per-stream dollar amounts is wrong. OPTIONAL: geographic mix (U.S. 62%, Japan 25% of FY2026 revenue).", "value": 1177253000, "unit": "USD", "period": "FY2026"}, "source": {"doc_id": "BOX_10K_FY2026", "quote": "To date, practically all of our revenue has been derived from subscription and premier services.", "quotes": [{"doc_id": "BOX_10K_FY2026", "quote": "We derive our revenue primarily from three sources: (1) subscription revenue, which is comprised of subscription fees from customers who have access to our ICM platform including routine customer support; (2) revenue from customers purchasing our premier services package; and"}, {"doc_id": "BOX_10K_FY2026", "quote": "Professional services revenue was not material as a percentage of total revenue for all periods presented."}, {"doc_id": "BOX_10K_FY2026", "quote": "Revenue | $1,177,253 | $1,090,130 | $1,037,741"}]}, "verified": "claude-draft", "note": "The 10-K has no revenue-disaggregation-by-stream table; the per-stream dollar amounts genuinely are not disclosed. Judge should reward stating that, not penalize it."}
{"id": "BOX:rev_nrr", "ticker": "BOX", "qid": "rev_nrr", "answer_type": "percent", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "Yes. Box discloses a net retention rate (Total ARR from customers 12 months earlier, including expansion, for customers subscribed at least 12 months). Most recent: 106% as of July 31, 2026 (Q2 FY2027), up from 103% as of July 31, 2025. MUST: 106% as of July 31, 2026. OPTIONAL: prior values (105% at Apr 30, 2026; 104% at Jan 31, 2026) and the definition. Reporting 104% (FY2026 10-K) as the most recent value is outdated/incorrect.", "value": 106, "unit": "percent", "period": "2026-07-31"}, "source": {"doc_id": "BOX_10Q_Q2FY2027", "quote": "Our net retention rate was 106% and 103% as of July 31, 2026 and 2025, respectively.", "quotes": [{"doc_id": "BOX_8K_Q2FY2027", "quote": "Net retention rate improved to 106%"}]}, "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "BOX:rev_arr", "ticker": "BOX", "qid": "rev_arr", "answer_type": "currency", "grade": "abstain", "expected": {"not_found": true, "text": "Box does not disclose an ARR dollar amount in any of the filings. It only defines 'Total ARR' (annualized recurring revenue from all active customer contracts at period end) as an input to its net retention rate (106% as of July 31, 2026). Related disclosed metrics: RPO of $1.7 billion and billings of $309.5 million for Q2 FY2027 (quarter ended July 31, 2026)."}, "source": {"method": "grep across all eight BOX filings (BOX_10K_FY2026, BOX_10K_FY2025, BOX_10Q_Q2FY2027, BOX_10Q_Q1FY2027, BOX_10Q_Q3FY2026, BOX_10Q_Q2FY2026, BOX_8K_Q2FY2027, BOX_DEF14A_2026) for 'ARR', 'annual recurring revenue', 'annualized recurring revenue', 'recurring revenue', 'Total ARR' with a value; only hits are the Total ARR definition inside the net retention rate paragraph (10-K FY2026, 10-K FY2025, all four 10-Qs); the 8-K key metrics list (billings, RPO, FCF) has no ARR figure.", "doc_id": "BOX_10K_FY2026", "quote": "We define Total ARR as the annualized recurring revenue from all active customer contracts at the end of a reporting period."}, "verified": "claude-draft", "note": "Conditional question; ARR is defined but no ARR amount is disclosed, so abstain per the rules."}
{"id": "BOX:rev_customers", "ticker": "BOX", "qid": "rev_customers", "answer_type": "count", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "Over 100,000 paying organizations as of January 31, 2026 (the most recent count disclosed; the FY2027 10-Qs and Q2 FY2027 8-K give no updated count). Box defines paying organizations as separate and distinct buying entities (e.g., a company, an educational or government institution, or a distinct business unit of a large corporation) that have entered into a subscription agreement with Box. MUST: 'over 100,000' paying organizations and the buying-entity definition. OPTIONAL: offered in 25 languages; FY2025 figure was also over 100,000.", "value": 100000, "unit": "count", "period": "2026-01-31"}, "source": {"doc_id": "BOX_10K_FY2026", "quote": "As of January 31, 2026, we had over 100,000 paying organizations, and our solution was offered in 25 languages.", "quotes": [{"doc_id": "BOX_10K_FY2026", "quote": "We define paying organizations as separate and distinct buying entities, such as a company, an educational or government institution, or a distinct business unit of a large corporation, that have entered into a subscription agreement with us to utilize our services."}]}, "verified": "claude-draft", "note": "Value is a lower bound ('over 100,000')."}
{"id": "BOX:rev_concentration", "ticker": "BOX", "qid": "rev_concentration", "answer_type": "yes_no", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "No. No single customer represented 10% or more of Box's revenue in FY2026 (year ended Jan 31, 2026), nor in FY2025 or FY2024. MUST: answer no for FY2026. OPTIONAL: no customer exceeded 10% of accounts receivable at Jan 31, 2026 (two resellers did at Jan 31, 2025)."}, "source": {"doc_id": "BOX_10K_FY2026", "quote": "No single customer represented 10% or more of our revenue in the year ended January 31, 2026.", "quotes": [{"doc_id": "BOX_10K_FY2026", "quote": "No single customer represented over 10% of revenue in the years ended January 31, 2026, 2025 and 2024."}]}, "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "BOX:rev_international", "ticker": "BOX", "qid": "rev_international", "answer_type": "percent", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "About 38% of FY2026 revenue came from customers outside the United States (derived: revenue attributable to U.S. customers was 62% for the year ended Jan 31, 2026). Japan alone was 25% of revenue. MUST: ~38% (or equivalently U.S. = 62%). OPTIONAL: Japan 25%; trend (U.S. share 64% in FY2025, 66% in FY2024). Do NOT accept ~40% (that is the share of contracts denominated in foreign currencies) or ~35% (the Q2 FY2027 press-release approximation) as the FY2026 answer.", "value": 38, "unit": "percent", "period": "FY2026"}, "source": {"doc_id": "BOX_10K_FY2026", "quote": "For the years ended January 31, 2026, 2025 and 2024, revenue attributable to customers in the U.S. was 62%, 64% and 66%, respectively."}, "verified": "claude-draft", "note": "38% is derived as 100% - 62%; the filing does not state 'outside the U.S.' directly for FY2026."}
{"id": "BOX:op_nongaap_quarter", "ticker": "BOX", "qid": "op_nongaap_quarter", "answer_type": "currency", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "Non-GAAP operating income for Q2 FY2027 (three months ended July 31, 2026) was $94.5 million ($94,513 thousand), a 29.4% non-GAAP operating margin, up from $84.0 million (28.6%) in Q2 FY2026. MUST: ~$94.5 million for the quarter ended July 31, 2026. OPTIONAL: 29.4% margin; prior-year comparison; GAAP operating income of $32.6 million; main add-back is $61.4 million of stock-based compensation. The six-month figure ($179.2 million) is not the answer.", "value": 94513000, "unit": "USD", "period": "Q2 FY2027 (quarter ended 2026-07-31)"}, "source": {"doc_id": "BOX_8K_Q2FY2027", "quote": "Record non-GAAP operating income of $94.5 million, or 29.4% of revenue, up from $84.0 million, or 28.6% of revenue.", "quotes": [{"doc_id": "BOX_8K_Q2FY2027", "quote": "Non-GAAP operating income and operating margin | $94,513 | 29.4% | $83,985 | 28.6% | $179,176 | 28.6% | $153,755 | 27.0%"}]}, "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "BOX:cf_fcf", "ticker": "BOX", "qid": "cf_fcf", "answer_type": "text", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "Yes, Box reports non-GAAP free cash flow, defined as cash flows from operating activities less net capital expenditures (purchases of property and equipment less proceeds from sales of property and equipment), principal payments of finance lease liabilities, capitalized software (development) costs, and other items that did not or are not expected to require cash settlement and that management considers outside the core business. FY2026 (year ended Jan 31, 2026) non-GAAP free cash flow was $312.9 million ($312,922 thousand), up 3% from $304.6 million in FY2025. MUST: the definition (OCF less net capex, finance lease principal payments and capitalized software) and ~$312.9 million for FY2026. OPTIONAL: reconciliation: OCF $356.5M - purchases of P&E $6.1M + proceeds $0.3M - capitalized internal-use software $37.8M.", "value": 312922000, "unit": "USD", "period": "FY2026"}, "source": {"doc_id": "BOX_10K_FY2026", "quote": "For the year ended January 31, 2026, our non-GAAP free cash flow was $312.9 million, a 3% increase from our non-GAAP free cash flow of $304.6 million for the year ended January 31, 2025.", "quotes": [{"doc_id": "BOX_10K_FY2026", "quote": "We define non-GAAP free cash flow as cash flows from operating activities less net capital expenditures (purchases of property and equipment less proceeds from sales of property and equipment), principal payments of finance lease liabilities, capitalized software costs,"}, {"doc_id": "BOX_10K_FY2026", "quote": "Non-GAAP free cash flow | $312,922 | $304,606 | $268,966"}]}, "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "BOX:debt_instruments", "ticker": "BOX", "qid": "debt_instruments", "answer_type": "list", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "At January 31, 2026 Box's only funded debt was its 1.50% convertible senior notes due September 15, 2029 ('2029 Convertible Notes'): $460.0 million principal outstanding (net carrying amount $451.0 million after $9.0 million unamortized issuance costs), interest 1.50% per year payable semiannually (Mar 15/Sep 15), maturity September 15, 2029, conversion price ~$43.46/share. Box also has a $75.0 million secured revolving credit facility (June 2023 Facility, as amended Dec 2024; $45.0 million letter-of-credit sublimit) with NO borrowings outstanding; revolving loans bear interest at term SOFR or daily simple SOFR + 1.35%-1.85% (or prime + 0.35%-0.85%), maturing June 30, 2028 (earlier in certain circumstances, e.g., Feb 11, 2028 if Series A preferred remains outstanding). The 0.00% 2026 Convertible Notes ($205.0 million remaining principal) matured and were settled in cash on January 15, 2026, so they were not outstanding at year-end. MUST: the 2029 notes with $460.0M principal, 1.50%, Sept 15, 2029 maturity; the revolver with $0 drawn. MUST NOT list the 2026 notes as outstanding. OPTIONAL: letters of credit $12.3M; Series A convertible preferred ($500M, 3% dividend) is mezzanine equity, not debt.", "value": 460000000, "unit": "USD", "period": "2026-01-31"}, "source": {"doc_id": "BOX_10K_FY2026", "quote": "In September 2024, we issued $460.0 million aggregate principal amount of 1.50% convertible senior notes due September 15, 2029.", "quotes": [{"doc_id": "BOX_10K_FY2026", "quote": "Principal | $460,000 | $— | $460,000 | $205,000"}, {"doc_id": "BOX_10K_FY2026", "quote": "On January 15, 2026, we settled the outstanding $205.0 million aggregate principal amount in cash and the conversion premium by issuing 1.1 million shares of our Class A common stock."}, {"doc_id": "BOX_10K_FY2026", "quote": "As of January 31, 2026, we had no debt outstanding on the June 2023 Facility and were in compliance with all financial covenants."}, {"doc_id": "BOX_10K_FY2026", "quote": "a margin ranging from 1.35% to 1.85%, (b) a daily simple SOFR rate plus a margin ranging from 1.35% to 1.85%, or (c) a prime rate plus a margin of 0.35% to 0.85%."}]}, "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "BOX:comp_competitors", "ticker": "BOX", "qid": "comp_competitors", "answer_type": "list", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "Named competitors: in content management, Microsoft (SharePoint) and OpenText (Documentum); in enterprise file sync and share, Microsoft (OneDrive), Google (Drive) and, to a lesser extent, Dropbox. Categories: traditional on-premise content management vendors (records management, business process workflow, archival); newer enterprise vendors entering content collaboration; simple file sync-and-share vendors (on-premises, hybrid or SaaS); social collaboration vendors; plus companies in e-signature, content collaboration, workflow automation, AI, and security and governance markets. MUST: Microsoft, OpenText, Google and Dropbox (with product context optional) and at least the general categories. OPTIONAL: principal competitive factors; companies' internally developed solutions.", "period": "FY2026"}, "source": {"doc_id": "BOX_10K_FY2026", "quote": "In the content management market, our primary competitors include, but are not limited to, Microsoft (SharePoint) and OpenText (Documentum). In the enterprise file sync and share market, our primary competitors include, but are not limited to, Microsoft (OneDrive), Google (Drive) and, to a lesser extent, Dropbox.", "quotes": [{"doc_id": "BOX_10K_FY2026", "quote": "We face competition from a broad spectrum of technology providers: traditional content management vendors who deploy on-premise and offer deep records management, business process workflow, and archival capabilities;"}, {"doc_id": "BOX_10K_FY2026", "quote": "With our expanded product offerings and use cases, we also now compete with companies in the e-signature, content collaboration, workflow automation, AI, and security and governance markets."}]}, "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "BOX:risk_key", "ticker": "BOX", "qid": "risk_key", "answer_type": "list", "grade": "support_only", "expected": {"text": "Reference picks (judgment question; only citation support is scored): (1) Competition from much larger platform vendors - Microsoft (SharePoint/OneDrive), Google (Drive), OpenText and Dropbox - some of which bundle content products for free or at low prices in enterprise license deals, pressuring Box's pricing, with generative AI potentially reshaping the market. (2) Dependence on renewals and expansion: results hinge on customers renewing and upgrading (seat expansion, multi-product Suites such as Enterprise Advanced); net retention rate has fluctuated (104% at Jan 31, 2026 vs 102% a year earlier) amid budget scrutiny and partial churn. (3) Foreign-currency exposure concentrated in Japan: ~25% of revenue comes from Japanese customers and ~40% of revenue is in foreign-currency contracts, so yen weakness directly hurts reported results despite hedging. Other defensible company-specific picks: reliance on third-party public cloud hosting after migrating off its own data centers; rights and redemption obligations of the KKR-led Series A convertible preferred; AI-related risks."}, "source": {"doc_id": "BOX_10K_FY2026", "quote": "Some of our competitors offer their products or services at lower prices or for free as part of a broader bundled product sale or enterprise license arrangement, which has placed pricing pressure on our business.", "quotes": [{"doc_id": "BOX_10K_FY2026", "quote": "Our primary competitors in the enterprise content management market include Microsoft (SharePoint) and OpenText (Documentum). In the enterprise file sync and share market, our primary competitors include Microsoft (OneDrive), Google (Drive) and, to a lesser extent, Dropbox."}, {"doc_id": "BOX_10K_FY2026", "quote": "Our business depends substantially on customers renewing their subscriptions with us and expanding their use of our services. Any decline in our customer renewals or failure to convince our customers to broaden their use of our services would harm our future operating results."}, {"doc_id": "BOX_10K_FY2026", "quote": "Fluctuations in the relative value of the U.S. dollar and foreign currencies, particularly the Japanese Yen, and to a lesser extent, the British Pound, the Euro, and the Polish Zloty, have in the past and may in the future negatively impact our operating results."}, {"doc_id": "BOX_10K_FY2026", "quote": "For the years ended January 31, 2026, 2025 and 2024 revenue attributable to customers in Japan was 25%, 23% and 21%, respectively."}]}, "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "BOX:mgmt_ceo", "ticker": "BOX", "qid": "mgmt_ceo", "answer_type": "text", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "Aaron Levie, Box's co-founder, has been Chief Executive Officer (and a director) since April 2005. He also served as Chair of the Board from December 2013 to May 2021. He remains CEO as of the latest filings (Q2 FY2027 8-K dated Aug 25, 2026 and 10-Q filed Aug 26, 2026); no leadership change is disclosed. MUST: Aaron Levie; CEO since April 2005 (i.e., since founding). OPTIONAL: co-founder; former Chair 2013-2021; independent Chair is Bethany Mayer."}, "source": {"doc_id": "BOX_DEF14A_2026", "quote": "Aaron Levie co-founded our company and has served as our Chief Executive Officer and a member of our Board of Directors since April 2005.", "quotes": [{"doc_id": "BOX_8K_Q2FY2027", "quote": "said Aaron Levie, co-founder and CEO of Box."}]}, "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "BOX:comp_ceo_total", "ticker": "BOX", "qid": "comp_ceo_total", "answer_type": "currency", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "CEO Aaron Levie's total compensation in the Summary Compensation Table for fiscal year 2026 (year ended Jan 31, 2026) was $257,076: salary $162,000, no stock awards, non-equity incentive plan compensation $94,912, all other compensation $164. MUST: $257,076 for FY2026. MUST NOT give the FY2025 total ($12,608,146, which included a $12.35M PSU grant). OPTIONAL: components; Levie declined FY2026 equity awards.", "value": 257076, "unit": "USD", "period": "FY2026"}, "source": {"doc_id": "BOX_DEF14A_2026", "quote": "Aaron Levie Chief Executive Officer | 2026 | 162,000 | — | — | 94,912 | 164 | 257,076", "quotes": [{"doc_id": "BOX_DEF14A_2026", "quote": "2025 | 162,000 | — | 12,353,800 (4) | 92,070 | 276 | 12,608,146"}]}, "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "BOX:comp_bonus_metrics", "ticker": "BOX", "qid": "comp_bonus_metrics", "answer_type": "list", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "Annual cash bonus (Fiscal 2026 Executive Bonus Plan): two equally weighted (50/50) corporate metrics - GAAP revenue and non-GAAP operating income (FY2026 targets $1,157.5M and $324.1M; payout 105.5% of target, paid half in cash and half in fully vested RSUs). Performance-based equity: PSUs granted to the COO and CFO in FY2026 are earned on the same two metrics - annual revenue (50%) and non-GAAP operating income (50%) goals for FY2026 (earned at 107.9% of target, then time-vest over three years). The CEO received no FY2026 equity award; his outstanding PSUs (600,000 granted in FY2025) vest only on stock-price thresholds of $40, $50 and $60. MUST: revenue and non-GAAP operating income for both the bonus and the (non-CEO) PSUs. OPTIONAL: CEO PSUs tied to stock price hurdles; weights, thresholds and payout percentages."}, "source": {"doc_id": "BOX_DEF14A_2026", "quote": "Payouts under our Fiscal 2026 Executive Bonus Plan are based on achievement of revenue and non-GAAP operating income targets. PSUs to CEO are based on achievement of stock price targets and PSUs to other Named Executive Officers are based on achievement of revenue and non-GAAP operating income targets.", "quotes": [{"doc_id": "BOX_DEF14A_2026", "quote": "our Compensation Committee selected revenue and non-GAAP operating income as those measures were deemed as best supporting the achievement of our annual operating plan and enhancing long-term value creation."}, {"doc_id": "BOX_DEF14A_2026", "quote": "the PSUs granted to Mr. Levie in fiscal year 2025, which become eligible to vest only if stock price thresholds of $40, $50 and $60 are achieved"}]}, "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "BOX:comp_sayonpay", "ticker": "BOX", "qid": "comp_sayonpay", "answer_type": "percent", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "Approximately 96% of votes cast approved the say-on-pay proposal at Box's 2025 annual meeting (the most recent vote disclosed in the 2026 proxy). MUST: ~96% (2025 annual meeting). OPTIONAL: 98% in 2023 and 2024; votes are held annually. Results of the June 25, 2026 meeting are not in the corpus.", "value": 96, "unit": "percent", "period": "2025 annual meeting"}, "source": {"doc_id": "BOX_DEF14A_2026", "quote": "We conducted a Say-on-Pay vote at our 2025 annual meeting of stockholders. Approximately 96% of the votes cast by stockholders were in favor of approving the compensation of our named executive officers.", "quotes": [{"doc_id": "BOX_DEF14A_2026", "quote": "In 2023 and 2024, 98% of our stockholders voted in favor of our say-on-pay proposal, and in 2025, 96% voted in favor."}]}, "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "BOX:gov_board", "ticker": "BOX", "qid": "gov_board", "answer_type": "text", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "The board has eight directors, seven of whom are independent under NYSE standards; the only non-independent director is CEO Aaron Levie. MUST: 8 directors, 7 independent. OPTIONAL: independent Chair Bethany Mayer (separate Chair/CEO); independent directors are Sue Barsamian, Dana Evan, Jack Lazar, Dan Levin, Bethany Mayer, Steve Murphy and Amit Walia; board is classified into three classes."}, "source": {"doc_id": "BOX_DEF14A_2026", "quote": "Our business affairs are managed under the direction of our Board of Directors, which is currently composed of eight members. Seven of our eight directors are independent within the meaning of the listing standards of the New York Stock Exchange."}, "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "BOX:gov_structure", "ticker": "BOX", "qid": "gov_structure", "answer_type": "text", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "Yes, the board is classified: directors are divided into three staggered classes, each elected for three-year terms (Class III nominees in 2026 for terms to 2029). No multi-class common structure: Box has a single class of common stock (Class A, one vote per share; the former Class B common stock was retired in 2018). The only other voting security is the Series A Convertible Preferred Stock (KKR-led), which votes together with Class A on an as-converted basis and has certain separate class consent rights. MUST: classified/staggered board = yes; single class of common stock with one vote per share = no dual-class. OPTIONAL: the Series A preferred voting, the anti-takeover framing in risk factors."}, "source": {"doc_id": "BOX_DEF14A_2026", "quote": "Our Board of Directors is divided into three staggered classes of directors. At each annual meeting of stockholders, a class of directors will be elected for a three-year term to succeed the same class whose term is then expiring.", "quotes": [{"doc_id": "BOX_10K_FY2026", "quote": "The holder of each share of Class A common stock is entitled to 1 vote per share."}, {"doc_id": "BOX_10Q_Q2FY2027", "quote": "authorizing a classified board of directors whose members serve staggered three-year terms;"}, {"doc_id": "BOX_DEF14A_2026", "quote": "Each stockholder of record is entitled to one vote per share of Class A common stock and one vote per each share of Class A common stock underlying a share of Series A Preferred Stock on an “as converted” basis."}]}, "verified": "claude-draft"}
{"id": "BOX:gov_activist", "ticker": "BOX", "qid": "gov_activist", "answer_type": "text", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "No cooperation, settlement or standstill agreement with an activist investor is disclosed in any of the filings. The only related arrangement is the April 7, 2021 Investment Agreement with a KKR-led investor group (purchasers of $500 million of Series A Convertible Preferred Stock; KKR entities hold ~28.5% of the preferred): it gave KKR the right to designate one candidate for nomination to Box's Board (while KKR kept minimum holdings) plus preemptive rights, but KKR has since waived both its board-designation right and its preemptive rights (per the latest 10-Q). Holders of a majority of the Series A preferred must consent to certain actions (e.g., issuing senior/parity securities, special dividends above an agreed amount). MUST: say there is no activist cooperation/standstill agreement AND mention the KKR investment agreement's board nomination right and that it has been waived. An answer that only says 'none' without the KKR nuance is incomplete but directionally correct. Also correct: an answer stating that no activist cooperation/standstill agreement is disclosed (the question is ambiguous for Box; KKR is an investor, not an activist)."}, "source": {"doc_id": "BOX_10Q_Q2FY2027", "quote": "however, KKR has waived both its rights to designate or nominate an Investor Designee (as defined in the Investment Agreement) to the Board and its preemptive rights pursuant to the Investment Agreement.", "quotes": [{"doc_id": "BOX_10Q_Q2FY2027", "quote": "KKR had the right to designate one candidate for nomination for election to our Board of Directors and preemptive rights to participate in certain future offering of securities of Box for so long as KKR and its permitted transferees maintained a minimum aggregate holdings of our stock"}, {"doc_id": "BOX_10Q_Q2FY2027", "quote": "In May 2021, we issued 500,000 shares of our Series A Convertible Preferred Stock to a group of investors led by KKR."}]}, "verified": "claude-draft", "note": "No activist agreement (no Starboard etc.) appears anywhere in the corpus; searched 'standstill', 'cooperation agreement', 'nomination agreement', 'settlement agreement', 'activist', 'Starboard', 'designee'. The KKR nomination right is the only shareholder agreement with board rights; the 10-K FY2026 says only the designation right was waived, the Q2 FY2027 10-Q adds that preemptive rights were also waived. Human reviewer: decide whether a bare 'none' should get credit (consider accept_not_found).", "accept_not_found": true}
{"id": "BOX:gov_holders", "ticker": "BOX", "qid": "gov_holders", "answer_type": "list", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "Per the 2026 proxy (as of May 1, 2026; 138,532,634 Class A shares outstanding), holders of more than 5% of Class A common stock: BlackRock, Inc. - 23,362,713 shares (16.9%); Vanguard Portfolio Management - 11,413,812 shares (8.2%); Earnest Partners, LLC - 7,734,494 shares (5.6%); Vanguard Capital Management - 7,311,369 shares (5.3%). MUST: BlackRock, Vanguard (both Vanguard entities or Vanguard in total ~13.5%) and Earnest Partners, with approximate percentages. OPTIONAL: >5% holders of the Series A Convertible Preferred - KKR affiliates (28.5% of the preferred), Soros Fund Management (13.3%), Hudson Bay Capital (8.0%) - none of which owns more than 5% of the Class A shares; listing them as >5% common holders is wrong."}, "source": {"doc_id": "BOX_DEF14A_2026", "quote": "BlackRock, Inc. (1) | 23,362,713 | 16.9% | — | —", "quotes": [{"doc_id": "BOX_DEF14A_2026", "quote": "Vanguard Portfolio Management (2)(3) | 11,413,812 | 8.2% | — | —"}, {"doc_id": "BOX_DEF14A_2026", "quote": "Vanguard Capital Management (2)(4) | 7,311,369 | 5.3% | — | —"}, {"doc_id": "BOX_DEF14A_2026", "quote": "Earnest Partners, LLC (5) | 7,734,494 | 5.6% | — | —"}, {"doc_id": "BOX_DEF14A_2026", "quote": "+ None of the holders of Series A Preferred Shares beneficially owns more than 5% of the Class A Shares."}]}, "verified": "claude-draft", "note": "Vanguard reports on a disaggregated basis after a March 2026 internal realignment; accept either the two entities separately or combined."}
{"id": "BOX:lit_material", "ticker": "BOX", "qid": "lit_material", "answer_type": "text", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "No. Box does not disclose any material pending legal proceedings. Its filings (latest: 10-Q for the quarter ended July 31, 2026) say it is subject from time to time to ordinary-course litigation and claims, and that it believes there was not at least a reasonable possibility it had incurred a material loss from such contingencies as of July 31, 2026. MUST: no material pending proceedings / only ordinary-course matters with no reasonably possible material loss. OPTIONAL: small litigation expenses are excluded from non-GAAP results ($1.5M in FY2026)."}, "source": {"doc_id": "BOX_10Q_Q2FY2027", "quote": "Although the results of litigation and claims cannot be predicted with certainty, we believe there was not at least a reasonable possibility that we had incurred a material loss with respect to such loss contingencies as of July 31, 2026.", "quotes": [{"doc_id": "BOX_10K_FY2026", "quote": "From time to time, we are subject to litigation and claims that arise in the ordinary course of business."}]}, "verified": "claude-draft", "accept_not_found": true, "note": "Filings do not literally say 'not a party to any material legal proceedings'; 'none' is implied by the loss-contingency language."}
{"id": "BOX:ma_recent", "ticker": "BOX", "qid": "ma_recent", "answer_type": "list", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "In the two most recent fiscal years (FY2025 and FY2026, Feb 1, 2024 - Jan 31, 2026) the only acquisition disclosed is Box's August 2024 (FY2025) acquisition of the AI-powered intelligent document processing technology of Alphamoon; no purchase consideration is disclosed (not separately reported, immaterial). No acquisitions are disclosed for FY2026 (only $0.6 million of acquisition-related expenses excluded from non-GAAP results). MUST: Alphamoon technology acquisition (Aug 2024) and that consideration is not disclosed; no FY2026 acquisitions disclosed. OPTIONAL: Crooze Corporation acquired December 2023 (FY2024, outside the window). An answer that invents a purchase price is wrong."}, "source": {"doc_id": "BOX_10K_FY2025", "quote": "For example, in December 2023, we acquired Crooze Corporation, a provider of no-code enterprise content management applications built on the Box platform, and in August 2024, we acquired the intelligent document processing technology of Alphamoon.", "quotes": [{"doc_id": "BOX_10K_FY2025", "quote": "Box acquired the AI-powered, intelligent document processing technology of Alphamoon."}, {"doc_id": "BOX_10K_FY2026", "quote": "Acquisition-related expenses | 592 | — | 378 | — | 120 | —"}]}, "verified": "claude-draft", "note": "Searched all filings for 'acquired', 'acquisition of', 'business combination', 'purchase consideration', 'purchase price', 'Alphamoon', 'Crooze'; no consideration amount appears anywhere. Goodwill rose $76.97M -> $82.29M in FY2026 without any disclosed acquisition (cause not stated; possibly FX)."}
{"id": "BOX:cap_buyback_auth", "ticker": "BOX", "qid": "cap_buyback_auth", "answer_type": "text", "grade": "judged", "expected": {"text": "Box's Board has authorized an open-market share repurchase plan for Class A common stock that it has expanded several times: an additional $150 million announced March 4, 2025 (plan then expiring March 3, 2026); a $150 million expansion announced December 2, 2025 (plan then expiring December 1, 2026); and a $500 million expansion announced March 19, 2026, with the plan now expiring September 30, 2027. As of July 31, 2026 approximately $378 million remained authorized and available ($378.5 million per the issuer-purchases table). MUST: the March 2026 $500 million expansion and ~$378 million remaining as of July 31, 2026. OPTIONAL: earlier expansions; $59.2 million remaining at Jan 31, 2026; Q2 FY2027 repurchases of 2.6 million shares for ~$66 million.", "value": 378000000, "unit": "USD", "period": "2026-07-31"}, "source": {"doc_id": "BOX_10Q_Q2FY2027", "quote": "On March 19, 2026, we announced that our Board of Directors authorized a $500 million expansion of the share repurchase plan. The authorized repurchase plan will expire on September 30, 2027.", "quotes": [{"doc_id": "BOX_10Q_Q2FY2027", "quote": "As of July 31, 2026, approximately $378 million remained authorized and available for additional repurchases."}, {"doc_id": "BOX_8K_Q2FY2027", "quote": "As of July 31, 2026, approximately $378 million of buyback capacity was remaining under Box’s current share repurchase plan."}, {"doc_id": "BOX_10K_FY2026", "quote": "On December 2, 2025, we announced that our Board of Directors authorized a $150 million expansion of the share repurchase plan. The authorized repurchase plan will expire on December 1, 2026."}, {"doc_id": "BOX_10Q_Q2FY2026", "quote": "on March 4, 2025, we announced that our Board of Directors authorized the repurchase of an additional $150 million shares of our Class A common stock."}]}, "verified": "claude-draft"}
